      "question_number": 1,
      "question": "In Python, what is the key behavioral difference between a list and a tuple, and how do you create a one-item tuple?",
      "message_status": "complete",
      "answer": "- Lists are mutable while tuples are immutable . [S1]\n- Tuples cannot support item assignment, resulting in a TypeError if attempted . [S1]\n- Lists can contain mutable objects such as other lists . [S1]\n- Tuples are often used to represent heterogeneous sequences of related data . [S2]\n- Lists tend to hold a varying number of objects all of which have the same type . [S2]\n- Only immutable elements like tuples can be used as dictionary keys . [S2]\n- A one-item tuple is created by separating the item with a comma, for example: `t = 12345,` . [S1]",
