      "question_number": 2,
      "question": "In FastAPI, how do you declare a path parameter and a query parameter in an endpoint, and how does the framework distinguish them?",
      "message_status": "complete",
      "answer": "- FastAPI distinguishes path parameters from query parameters based on their type annotations and location in the function signature . [S1]\n- Path parameters are identified when a function parameter is declared to match a specific path segment . [S2]\n- Query parameters are interpreted as singular types such as `int`, `float`, `str`, or `bool` when not explicitly defined as part of the path or body . [S3]\n- FastAPI recognizes that a parameter is required if it lacks a default value, regardless of its type annotation . [S3]\n- Type annotations like `str | None` do not determine requirement status; instead, a default value of `None` indicates the parameter is optional . [S3]\n- Function parameters matching the path structure are extracted from the URL path by FastAPI . [S2]\n- Parameters declared as Pydantic models are extracted from the request body rather than the query string or path . [S2]\n- FastAPI can handle multiple body parameters simultaneously, validating and documenting them correctly within a single request . [S5]\n- Singular values declared without the `Body` annotation may be incorrectly interpreted as query parameters by FastAPI . [S6]\n- The `Body` annotation must be used to explicitly instruct FastAPI to treat a singular value as part of the request body . [S6]",
