      "question_number": 7,
      "question": "In PostgreSQL, what is a transaction, and what do COMMIT and ROLLBACK do to the changes made within it?",
      "message_status": "complete",
      "answer": "- A transaction in PostgreSQL is defined by surrounding SQL commands with BEGIN and COMMIT commands . [S1]\n- Each individual SQL statement executed without an explicit BEGIN command has an implicit BEGIN and (if successful) COMMIT wrapped around it . [S1]\n- The updates made by an open transaction are invisible to other transactions until the transaction completes . [S1]\n- Upon completion, all updates made by a transaction become visible simultaneously . [S1]\n- Issuing the ROLLBACK command cancels all updates made so far within the transaction . [S1]\n- PostgreSQL treats every SQL statement as being executed within a transaction . [S1]\n- A group of statements surrounded by BEGIN and COMMIT is sometimes called a transaction block . [S1]\n- Savepoints allow for selectively discarding parts of a transaction while committing the rest . [S1]\n- ROLLBACK TO discards database changes between defining a savepoint and rolling back to it . [S1]\n- Changes earlier than a savepoint are kept after rolling back to that savepoint . [S1]\n- PostgreSQL supports a two-phase commit (2PC) protocol for distributed systems using PREPARE TRANSACTION, COMMIT PREPARED, and ROLLBACK PREPARED commands . [S2]",
      "retrieval_mode": "dense",
